WEBVTT

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:02.750
Oh

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:49.950
I suppose one of the secrets of success in war

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.830
has always been preparedness,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.390
anticipating the threat and knowing how to react to it,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.980
and where, and when, and then doing what you...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.390
had to do with surgical precision.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:26.090
The story of how we came to be able to use

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.610
all this massive firepower to such deadly effect.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.890
As usual, links the strangest things.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.040
Sheep and canals and breakfast food and department stores, for instance.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:45.890
And just this once, the story starts with the medieval equivalent of this tornado bomber.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.140
And the thing that makes them both unique in war.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:57.250
The supreme capability both of them have, for one thing above all, flexible response.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:22.350
Now that's flexibility, and back in the 13th century, the famous English longbow put the fear of God into anybody you pointed it at.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.940
The longbow was called long because it was six foot long,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.980
and it was a medieval terror weapon.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.000
It could skewer a knight to his horse at 400 yards

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.980
or put an arrow through a six-inch-thick oak door.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.140
Longbowmen were top-gun superstars,

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.400
and anything they wanted, they got.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.040
I mean, you were in a tight spot, and you said,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.900
I am an English longbowman.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.700
They'd even let you out of jail.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:10.180
Unless you were lying, which of course you were, because this wasn't English.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.750
That's a myth. This was Welsh.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:18.530
And speaking of myths, what about the greatest bowman of all, Robin Hood?

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.320
In spite of the fact that Robin Hood is the stuff of song and legend,

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.100
the truth is, he was real.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:41.770
Not Hollywood real.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.540
No band of merry men.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.540
Nothing to do with King Richard or the Sheriff of Nottingham or Sherwood Forest.

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:50.620
But he was a real outlaw in Yorkshire.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:52.600
So, no myth.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.400
The one who was, was Maid Marian.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.320
She certainly never existed.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:09.630
Maid Marian is a character invented in 1285 by a song and dance man from Flanders called Adam de la Halle,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.420
who is down here in Italy with his boss and his boss's army,

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.610
which is helping out the boss's cousin, the King of Naples.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:21.580
But, like all troops, the army is homesick and misses its girlfriends.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:29.740
So Adam raises morale with a little show all about some very slightly naughty goings-on back in Flanders.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.170
and starring Maid Marian.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.300
It's one of those little-girl-goes-to-big-city stories

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.200
that goes something like this.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.720
Act one, don't speak to strange men.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.940
Into the life of an innocent shepherdess

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.860
comes a smoothie from the bright lights.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.720
who in no time at all works his oily charm on the unsuspecting maiden,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.520
and before she knows it, Marion's in Act Two.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.580
Some things a girl shouldn't do.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.960
Having persuaded Marion to leave home,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.980
this devious creep inveigles her into trying a little night-time naughtiness.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.020
This is shocking stuff for a poor peasant girl who's never even heard of a bear midriff.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.520
Never mind all these people jumping around and drinking booze and whooping it up to the

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.780
strains of the 13th century equivalent of some very heavy rock.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.900
As the night wears on, the spell wears off.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.900
Finally, act three of our cautionary tale,

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:10.700
Marion gets the point.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.540
Realising in the nick of time how close she has come to a fate worse than death,

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.160
Marion decides to hightail it back to home

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.780
and her faithful boyfriend named Robin.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.430
And that's how Maid Marian came into existence.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:27.060
Nothing to do with Robin Hood at all.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:38.580
Just a soft-porn burlesque with a plot really based on social problems back in Flanders

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:41.100
between homespun villagers who raised the sheep

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.640
and smooth city types who made all the profits from the wool.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:48.920
This is Flanders. Flat, isn't it?

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:52.280
Which is why, back in the Middle Ages,

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.640
this was where all the action was if you were in the business of making a bit of money.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.150
Well, look at the place.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:06:59.730
On the edge of the North Sea...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.060
Rivers everywhere, so ships could get in and out easily,

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.450
all the way from the Mediterranean one way

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:06.790
to the Baltic the other.

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.190
You unload their cargoes,

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:11.780
and then you ship the stuff all over Europe,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.880
which is a piece of cake because it's flat.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:19.060
So Flanders was flat and also rich.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:43.210
The trouble with being flat, of course, was that every winter the place flooded and hundreds of people drowned.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:46.160
And more to the point, so did hundreds of sheep.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:53.040
And wool was by far the country's biggest export, which is why Flanders was keen to become less wet

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:59.970
and why hydraulic engineers, like this guy, Simon Stevin, got busy in the 16th century, designed...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:13.960
systems to drain the land and keep it drained with windmills. Devon did so well. There were

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:18.700
soon more sheep than people and a booming economy and the kind of problem you get when

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:23.620
the country's made enough money to lend, which is when compound interest enters your life.

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.980
Now, I know you didn't switch on to see a programme about compound interest, but bear with...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.830
me because it'll only take a minute. See, compound interest is always about fractions of an amount

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:41.000
and never the whole thing, right? But adding and subtracting fractions is so complicated,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:46.680
it's a load of double dutch to most people. Quite a problem, even for people like Stevan.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:56.390
So one day, he went home and solved the problem. Basically, what he did was this.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.980
See, this sum of fractions adds up to...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.400
14 and 43 thousandths.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:06.910
Stevan changed the way you wrote fractions.

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:16.370
So 14 and 43 thousandths became the decimal fraction 14.043.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:22.110
Made compound interest to SNP and life easier for accountants.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:24.260
And changed America.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.970
Because I know it's obvious if you think about it,

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.970
but when you have revolutions...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.980
and set up a new country and a new government.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.090
What do you do with all the old stuff?

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.330
Okay, old government you chuck out.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:40.460
But what, for instance, about old money?

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:46.030
I mean, in 1782, American money was this.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:47.640
Well, ours.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.580
English guineas, shillings and pence,

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:59.080
and French sous, French levers, Spanish doubloons.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.680
Portuguese mordeiras, or anything else anybody would take.

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:07.280
And each one of them was worth something different

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:11.440
if you went from New York to South Carolina to Georgia to Pennsylvania.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.020
There was no such thing as American money in 1782,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.680
which was the year a one-legged, fun-loving New York socialite

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.980
called Gouverneur Morris took up a Simon Stevin suggestion

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:26.920
that everybody else had ignored for the first ever decimal coinage.

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:29.970
By the time President Jefferson had finished...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.180
around with Morris' idea, it had become dollars and cents, written with a symbol for Spanish pesos

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:45.950
and centavos. Still took Americans 50 years to give up using their English shillings.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:51.080
Of course, once Jefferson got involved, everybody forgot it was Morris who started it,

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:57.440
and that might have been that for him. Except, eventually, Morris did make his mark on history,

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.760
in an eerie way.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.840
In 1825, at the grand opening ceremony,

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.800
when they poured a keg of Great Lakes water into the Atlantic

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.740
to symbolise the joining of the two,

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.460
the Erie Canal was all thanks to Morris,

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:21.550
who'd headed the commission to build it.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.400
Greatest transportation system in America,

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:28.160
till they built this, right next to the canal,

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:29.880
which drove it out of business.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.920
because trains changed the way business was done.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:52.730
Of course, it made good sense to run the railroad tracks alongside the canal

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.470
because canal land is flat.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:57.740
But look at the tracks, single tracks.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:11:59.970
So how do you pass a train going the other way?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.990
other way, well, you sit in a siding till the other train comes by. How do you know it's coming? Well,

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:16.940
that's why you sit so long. You don't. Which is why the trains back then were never on time. And then in

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:29.970
1851, the Erie Railroad changed the world when it got itself organized with this. Telegraph solved the

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.800
single track problem at a stroke. Now trains knew they didn't have to wait around. So they ran more

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:42.210
trains. So the price of freight went down. So the American economy went up. The Telegraph got the

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:48.550
railroad people so well organized, everybody in business started copying their methods. The railroad

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:54.570
is where all this stuff started. Waybills, line managers, departments. Because running the

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:59.590
railroads was really complicated, with hundreds of points of sale and hundreds of thousands of

00:12:59.590 --> 00:12:59.890
customer.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.790
An organizational nightmare.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:05.320
Which is why one of the people running the Erie,

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:07.140
a fellow called McCallum, thank you,

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:11.150
organized the whole thing with the first management chart.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.500
Areas of responsibility, lines of communication,

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:21.110
data flow, personnel breakdown, all that.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.870
By the late 19th century, the railroads were running trains

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.970
right up to the door of American fa-

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:32.690
to bring in fuel and raw materials

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:35.590
and take away whatever they produced to be sold,

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:38.260
which left only one minor problem.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:39.600
So where?

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:43.960
The question was, what outlet was ever going to be big enough

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:46.640
to handle what American industry was churning out?

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:48.700
Everything from soup to nuts.

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:51.560
Answer, the department store.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:52.240
That's what.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:56.010
The place that changed the meaning of the word shopping.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.340
I mean, this wasn't just a place for getting everything you needed.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:03.190
Although getting everything you needed in one place was a new idea.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:06.740
No, this wasn't shopping. This was an experience.

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:14.570
It was glamour. Chandeliers, plush carpets, marble, beauty salons, day nurseries, charge accounts.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.200
This wasn't buying. This was consuming. And it was big.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:28.670
By 1877, the department stores were everywhere, persuading people to buy things they never knew they needed.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.250
And speaking of persuading people to buy,

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:35.240
that was the year some people in Ravenna, Ohio,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.340
started persuading consumers to eat,

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:39.040
something that up until then,

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:42.220
only horses and poverty-stricken Scotsmen had eaten.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.240
It was a product that brought together

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:46.040
everything happening in American industry.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:49.290
Railroads, management, consumerism,

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.040
raw materials in one end, finished goods out the other,

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:55.670
and one new vital ingredient, merchandising.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:57.870
This was the product.

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:19.690
Quaker came up with most of the persuaders you see everywhere today.

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.940
They gave away free gifts, they appealed to the housewife on a limited budget,

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.130
they even made a vaguely colonialist appeal, patriotism.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.680
Quaker also invented the idea of box-top coupons

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:35.940
and endorsements from consumers and stars like Shirley Temple.

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:39.400
So, with all this persuasion to consume,

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:44.010
about all there was left to do was persuade the workers to produce.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:46.420
Watch the closing gates.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.760
At this time, please remove your hands from the hornet so it may be checked for you.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:51.960
Motivation, it's called,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:55.260
and it's the only thing that could get me to do what I'm about to do.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.980
The idea of motivation all started with an experiment run at the West...

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.130
electric Hawthorne plant in Cicero, Illinois, between 1924 and 1927. And the best way to

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:11.640
appreciate the surprising results of the experiment is for you to be the psychologist.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:29.970
Okay, here's-

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.640
what you're trying to do. Find out what you have to do to get workers to produce more. So you explain

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:42.930
carefully that you're going to try upping the light levels. Production goes up. Then you explain

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:49.250
carefully you're going to lower the light levels. Production stays up. So you explain carefully

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:54.750
you're going to change everything at random. The heat, the working day, the length of the lunch break,

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:59.330
rest periods. You do that for five years and production goes up again.

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:03.710
Then you explain carefully you're going to put everything back where it was.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:05.720
Production stays up.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.190
Then you get the point.

00:17:09.330 --> 00:17:12.190
Explaining carefully made workers happy and more productive.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.800
Now, for some people, that fact was pretty hard to swallow.

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.460
But it was right up the street of a fellow called Cannon,

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:27.620
who spent years looking at what happens when you get hungry.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:29.740
He invented the barrier meal.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.500
you know, that shows up on x-rays,

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:33.900
to look at what happens when you eat something,

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:37.030
discovered that the food's moved along inside your stomach

00:17:37.030 --> 00:17:38.630
by a series of waves,

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:41.220
and that the waves and a dry mouth

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:42.690
are the sign that you're hungry.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.980
And then he discovered that when you get excited or scared,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.340
as I'm about to, the stomach waves stop,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.880
and a lot more happen.

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:13.420
Muscles!

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:25.810
Your liver!

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:36.540
Shannon realized that those were just the things your body needed when it was under extreme stress.

00:18:36.940 --> 00:18:40.930
That you could be ready to handle anything.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:51.220
Also discovered what happens to make you stop feeling all shook up.

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:59.900
The way after moments of extreme stress, like we're going through now, the body uses feedback to check the field.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:06.260
dangerous. And if it isn't, hunt out the necessary chemicals to bring all the levels back to normal.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:18.060
And you know what? Cannon was right. I can feel it happening. 20 years later,

00:19:18.540 --> 00:19:24.300
Cannon's feedback concept was transferred from biology to engineering, with deadly results.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:34.900
In the 1940s, feedback went into machines with the first early forms of automation in

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:39.090
artillery that used feedback from radar data to track the targets.

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:43.480
Then in the 1960s, the principle was used to operate robots in factories.

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:50.760
Today, the most spectacular form of feedback in action happens when this tornado does what

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:51.360
it does best.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.970
It's feedback from the aircraft's altimeter to the...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.650
onboard computer that lets the pilot concentrate on the mission while the plane flies itself

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:07.280
just above the ground.

00:20:16.680 --> 00:20:21.770
Feedback and flexible response could be the key to everything in the fast-changing world

00:20:21.770 --> 00:20:22.930
of the 21st century.

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:29.470
In the world of technology, the world of business, maybe the world of individual survival.

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:40.050
And most particularly, in the post-Cold War political world

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:43.630
of constantly shifting allegiances and threats and saber-rattling,

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:47.340
perhaps none of it holds together for long enough for ceasefires

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:49.780
or negotiations or peace plans

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:53.910
without the aid of one special kind of flexible response.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:00.140
Thank you.
